<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sandinista National Liberation Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as FSLN</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sandinista <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a> (FSLN) is a Nicaraguan political party founded in 1961 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> movement named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-cesar-sandino">Augusto César Sandino</a>. It overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a> in 1979 and governed until 1990, fighting the US-backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contras">Contras</a>, many of them based in Honduras. Its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daniel-ortega">Daniel Ortega</a> returned to power in 2007 and has ruled in an increasingly authoritarian way.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sandinista-national-liberation-front.jpg" alt="Sandinista National Liberation Front" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Inti; Stannered · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_FSLN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sandinistas took their name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augusto-cesar-sandino">Augusto César Sandino</a> (1895-1934), who led Nicaragua's nationalist rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> occupation of the country in the early 20th century. Sandino was assassinated on 21 February 1934 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a>, the US-equipped force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-garcia">Anastasio Somoza García</a>, whose family ruled the country from 1936 until the Sandinistas overthrew it in 1979.</p><p>The movement grew out of opposition and student groups of the late 1950s and early 1960s, with the universities of Léon and the National Autonomous University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a> as principal centres. Inspired by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuban-revolution">Cuban Revolution</a> and the FLN in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, it was founded in 1961 by Carlos Fonseca, Silvio Mayorga, Tomás Borge, Casimiro Sotelo and others as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-liberation-front-south-yemen">National Liberation Front</a>. Only Borge lived to see the victory of 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After a defeat at Pancasán in 1967 the Front adopted the doctrine of the Prolonged Popular War. The earthquake of 23 December 1972 destroyed much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/managua">Managua</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-somoza-debayle">Anastasio Somoza Debayle</a>'s National Guard embezzled much of the aid. The killing of the newspaper editor Pedro Joaquín Chamorro on 10 January 1978 set off riots and a general strike. By mid-April 1979 five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> fronts operated under the Front's joint command, and the Somoza regime fell in the Nicaraguan Revolution of that year.</p><p>The Sandinistas inherited a debt of US$1.6 billion, an estimated 30,000 to 50,000 war dead and 600,000 homeless. They first governed through a five-member Junta of National Reconstruction, and took exclusive power in March 1981. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, under President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a>, authorised the CIA to finance, arm and train the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/contras">Contras</a>, mostly remnants of Somoza's National Guard, and imposed a trade embargo in May 1985. Elections were held in 1984, and the war continued until 1989. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esquipulas-peace-agreement">Esquipulas II</a> treaty of 1987, the Sandinistas lost the election of 26 February 1990 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/violeta-chamorro">Violeta Barrios de Chamorro</a> and passed power peacefully.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>In government the Front carried out a literacy campaign, with Cuban teachers, that lowered illiteracy from 50% to 12%, a land reform affecting half of the country's arable land and a Ministry of Culture led by the poet and priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernesto-cardenal">Ernesto Cardenal</a>. Cuba also sent advisers, who numbered over 2,500. Critics reported detentions, censorship of the newspaper La Prensa and abuses against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito people</a>, while supporters and church delegations disputed some allegations as political. Its flag, red above black with the letters F S L N, is a modified version of Sandino's. Daniel Ortega was elected president in 2006, and held the office from 1985 to 1990 and again from 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sandinista National Liberation Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Honduras’s Cold War role as a base for US operations against <b>Sandinista</b> Nicaragua and the Salvadoran guerrillas, and later its position on cocaine routes from South America, gave it a strategic importance far beyond its size.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Sandinista</b> revolution of 1978–1979 destroyed the Guard, and in the 1980s the Sandinista Popular Army fought the US-backed Contra rebels in a war that killed some 30,000 people.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Republic of Nicaragua · Military</a></li></ul></section>
